ntdll: give back the busy count when cancelling queued threadpool work.

**Every cancelled queue entry leaked one busy worker, forever, and the pool
answered by creating a thread for every submit.** Caribbean Legend reached 388
threads in 14 seconds and died when the address space ran out.

Since Wine 5.12 (6d712a42ca, "ntdll: Increment num_busy_workers when queuing
TP object") `num_busy_workers` counts queue entries as well as running
callbacks: tp_object_prio_queue adds one when an object joins the queue, and
the worker takes it back after running the callback. That commit moved every
increment and decrement it could see. tp_object_cancel also removes an object
from the queue, and was not among them -- it unlinks the entry and zeroes
num_pending_callbacks, so no worker ever dequeues it and its count is never
returned. Upstream master is identical today.

The count only has to drift above num_workers once. tp_object_submit starts a
thread whenever `num_busy_workers >= num_workers`, so from then on every submit
creates one, whether or not any worker is actually busy, until max_workers
(500).

**What reaches it.** MSVC's parallel algorithms (std::for_each with
std::execution::par and friends) allocate a work object, post it once per
partition, run partitions on the calling thread, and then call
WaitForThreadpoolWorkCallbacks(work, TRUE) to cancel whatever the pool has not
started. The calling thread usually wins, so the cancel usually finds a queued
entry. Traced on device with +threadpool:

    engine.exe:0188  647 x TpAllocWork(callback 0x140196650)
                     8718 x TpPostWork, 647 x TpWaitForWork(.., 1)

    rounds   workers started   workers exited
       50               42                8
      200              113                8
      400              224                8
      647              361                8

Every round completes and not one worker exits: half a leaked thread per
round, linear, never recovered.

**Why it kills a game here and not on a PC.** A worker on Windows or Linux x86
costs its stack. Under FEX each one also reserves its JIT state -- a 511 MiB
region, a 246 MiB FEXMem_Lookup and a 16 MiB L1 table, about 1.3 GiB of
address space per thread. The 39-bit user address space is 512 GiB:

    10:03:12  threads  25  VmSize  50 GiB
    10:03:26  threads 384  VmSize 508 GiB
    allocate_virtual_memory out of memory for allocation, size 20000000
    libc: failed to allocate shadow stack: Out of memory

and the process is gone. The black screen is the same fault seen earlier: the
loading work that would have drawn the first frame is what spawns the threads.

The invariant this relies on holds under pool->cs: an object is linked into the
pool list exactly when num_pending_callbacks is non-zero (tp_object_execute
decrements it before the lock is released), so cancel drops exactly one
counted entry and gives back exactly one.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/threadpool.c b/dlls/ntdll/threadpool.c
index 4f707bdd782..e9489816e6d 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/threadpool.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/threadpool.c
@@ -2045,6 +2045,12 @@ static void tp_object_cancel( struct threadpool_object *object )
         object->num_pending_callbacks = 0;
         list_remove( &object->pool_entry );
 
+        /* The queue entry being dropped was counted busy by
+         * tp_object_prio_queue, and no worker will ever pick it up to give
+         * that count back. */
+        assert(pool->num_busy_workers);
+        pool->num_busy_workers--;
+
         if (object->type == TP_OBJECT_TYPE_WAIT)
             object->u.wait.signaled = 0;
     }
